class relativesortarray {
    public int[] relativeSortArray(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for(int i=0; i<arr2.length; i++) {
          for(int j=0; j<arr1.length; j++) {
               if(arr2[i] == arr1[j]) {
                result.add(arr1[j]);
                arr1[j] = -1;
               }     
            }
        }


          ArrayList<Integer> remaining = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int num : arr1) {
            if (num != -1) {
                remaining.add(num);
            }
        }

        Collections.sort(remaining);

        result.addAll(remaining);

       
        int[] ans = new int[result.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < result.size(); i++) {
            ans[i] = result.get(i);
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

  

//Time Complexity: O(n log n) where n is the
//  length of arr1. This is because we sort the
//  remaining elements of arr1 that are not in arr2.

//Space Complexity: O(n) where n is the length of arr1.
//  This is because we use an additional list to store the result 
//  and the remaining elements.


Time Complexity
🔹 Step 1: Nested Loop

Code structure:

for (i in arr2)
    for (j in arr1)
👉 How many times does it run?
Outer loop → runs m times (arr2.length)
Inner loop → runs n times (arr1.length)

So total operations:

n + n + n + ... (m times)
= n * m
💡 Why this happens

For every element in arr2, we scan the entire arr1 again

Example:

arr2 has 6 elements
arr1 has 11 elements

We scan arr1:

6 × 11 = 66 checks

Even if we already found elements, we still keep scanning → no optimization

🔹 Step 2: Collect remaining
for (num in arr1)

Runs:

O(n)
🔹 Step 3: Sorting remaining

Let remaining elements = k

Sorting takes:

O(k log k)
✅ Final Time Complexity
O(n * m + n + k log k)

We simplify:

n*m dominates
n is smaller than n*m

👉 Final:

O(n * m + k log k)
🚨 Worst Case

If almost all elements are not in arr2:

k ≈ n

So:

O(n*m + n log n)
📦 Space Complexity
What extra space are we using?
1. Result list

Stores all elements → size = n

O(n)
2. Remaining list

In worst case → all elements go here

O(n)
3. Ignoring input array (arr1)

We modify it in-place (-1 marking), so no extra array

✅ Final Space Complexity
O(n)
🧠 Intuition Summary
Why time is high?

Because:

We re-scan arr1 again and again
No memory of previous work
No direct access → only linear search

👉 That’s classic brute force inefficiency

Why space is O(n)?

Because:

We build a new result
We temporarily store leftover elements
⚡ One-line understanding
Time: slow because of repeated full scans → O(n*m)
Space: linear because we store output → O(n)

